#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"}] :question/id #uuid "2fb19e4b-c455-599f-8856-5bee85f8efc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-508" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n2x+3 = A(x+1)+B .\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+3 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ 1 & =0+B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+3 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 3 & =A+B \\\\ 3 & =A+1 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{2}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2x+3}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}+{(-\\frac{1}{x+1})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{1}{x+1}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1]-[\\frac{1}{2}-1] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/id #uuid "789c5233-a263-55a7-9b27-c834b2ceaf8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-509" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3}\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{A}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x-1)^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2x-1 & =A(3x-1)^{2}+B(3x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{3})-1 & =A{(3(\\frac{1}{3})-1)}^{2}+B(3(\\frac{1}{3})-1)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(0)}^{2}+2(0)-1 & =A{(0-1)}^{2}+B(0-1)+C \\\\ -1 & =A-B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ A-B & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get another equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(1)}^{2}+2(1)-1 & =A{(3(1)-1)}^{2}+B(3(1)-1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A+2B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 2A+B & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding our two equations $A-B=-\\dfrac{5}{3}$ and $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ 3A & =3 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+B & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ B & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3} = \\dfrac{1}{(3x-1)} +\\dfrac{8}{3(3x-1)^2} + \\dfrac{2}{3(3x-1)^3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{8}{3(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{3(3x-1)^{3}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x-1}\\text{d}x+\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{2}}\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|+\\frac{8}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3(3x-1)})+\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{6(3x-1)^{2}})+K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|-\\frac{8}{9(3x-1)}-\\frac{1}{9(3x-1)^{2}}+K\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "79049868-d542-5461-ab14-4a4336c9221d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7275" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{x}{x^2+2x+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{x^2+2x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac {A}{x+1} + \\dfrac {B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\nx= A(x+1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 0 & =A+B \\\\ 0 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac {1}{x+1} - \\dfrac {1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|{|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{1}{x+1}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+1|)+(\\frac{1}{1+1}-\\frac{1}{0+1}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "d9f5cd99-436c-5089-a699-b9f9ff245157" :question/math-academy-id "e-7298" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)}\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-5x+8}{(x-3)^{2}(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{(x-3)}+\\frac{B}{(x-3)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-3)^2(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5x+8 & =A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+1)+C(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({3}^{2})-5(3)+8 & =A(3-3)(3+1)+B(3+1)+C(3-3)^{2} \\\\ 20 & =B(4) \\\\ B & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)^{2}-5(-1)+8 & =A(-1-3)(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C(-1-3)^{2} \\\\ 16 & =C{(-4)}^{2} \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({0}^{2})-5(0)+8 & =A(0-3)(0+1)+B(0+1)+C(0-3)^{2} \\\\ 8 & =-3A+B+9C \\\\ 8 & =-3A+5+9(1) \\\\ 3A & =6 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)} = \\dfrac{2}{(x-3)} +\\dfrac{5}{(x-3)^2} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-5x+8}{(x-3)^{2}(x+1)}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{(x-3)}+\\frac{5}{(x-3)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+5(-\\,\\frac{1}{x-3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{5}{(x-3)}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77d97798-15c6-5612-a16c-52f50851ec27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{5}{x+2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317699} {:answer/id #uuid "370083bf-d47d-5481-85c7-928f8994c417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{x-3}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317700} {:answer/id #uuid "8a813ae3-2365-5a96-9cf8-49b38f1ba36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{3}{x-3}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317701} {:answer/id #uuid "71b8dedd-f14b-5310-8f93-cae819f37ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{x+2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317702} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd41a81-6db0-5276-8471-0b78f48e013d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{2}{x-3}+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d97798-15c6-5612-a16c-52f50851ec27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{5}{x+2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317699} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dad80b4-a31d-5d79-8a01-74c0d9355b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "749395dd-2c35-5c9b-b2d0-e5eb656fba89" :question/math-academy-id "q-11340" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5x+35}{(x+2)^{2}(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+35}{(x+2)^{2}(x-3)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+2)^{2}(x-3)$ and get\n\n$$\n5x+35=A(x+2)(x-3)+B(x-3)+C(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)+35 & =A(-2+2)(-2-3)+B(-2-3)+C(-2+2)^{2} \\\\ -10+35 & =B(-5) \\\\ B & =\\frac{25}{-5} \\\\ B & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3)+35 & =A(3+2)(3-3)+B(3-3)+C(3+2)^{2} \\\\ 15+35 & =C{(5)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{50}{25} \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)+35 & =A(0+2)(0-3)+B(0-3)+C(0+2)^{2} \\\\ 35 & =-6A-3B+4C \\\\ 35 & =-6A-3(-5)+4(2) \\\\ 35 & =-6A+23 \\\\ 6A & =-35+23 \\\\ A & =\\frac{-12}{6} \\\\ A & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+35}{(x+2)^{2}(x-3)}=-\\frac{2}{(x+2)}-\\frac{5}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+35}{(x+2)^{2}(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{(x+2)}-\\frac{5}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-3)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{5}{x+2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc68f114-a145-5857-ab06-d0c90378d2c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|-\\frac{2}{1-x}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317706} {:answer/id #uuid "178cb0a4-8945-594e-8f8b-60ef6c57aafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|-\\frac{3}{1-x}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317707} {:answer/id #uuid "5a724608-8e02-50d7-9012-c0bdce32187e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|-\\frac{2}{1-x}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317708} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6d9360-bbce-55bc-bd32-24cc5016c05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|+\\frac{3}{1-x}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317709} {:answer/id #uuid "59a2f51a-8ea5-5688-b3a0-71a8a6684752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|+\\frac{3}{1-x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59a2f51a-8ea5-5688-b3a0-71a8a6684752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|+\\frac{3}{1-x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317710} :answer-field/id #uuid "220e86b4-f643-5ab4-a2b9-e3f7771ec7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42759d43-e843-516a-a952-88c7ffb6134c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11369" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{(1-x)}+\\frac{B}{(1-x)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(1-x)^{2}(x+5)$ and get\n\n$$\n-9x+27=A(1-x)(x+5)+B(x+5)+C(1-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(1)+27 & =A(1-1)(1+5)+B(1+5)+C(1-1)^{2} \\\\ -9+27 & =B(6) \\\\ B & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(-5)+27 & =A(1-(-5))(-5+5)+B(-5+5)+C(1-(-5))^{2} \\\\ -45+27 & =C{(6)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{72}{36} \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(0)+27 & =A(1-0)(0+5)+B(0+5)+C(1-0)^{2} \\\\ 27 & =5A+5B+C \\\\ 27 & =5A+5(3)+2 \\\\ 27 & =5A+17 \\\\ 5A & =27-17 \\\\ A & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}=\\frac{2}{(1-x)}+\\frac{3}{(1-x)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{(1-x)}+\\frac{3}{(1-x)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|+\\frac{3}{1-x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38ab01e4-c77e-5e40-bc9f-a5a2b819ffd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+\\frac{2}{2x+3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317713} {:answer/id #uuid "c77abbfa-a836-5179-8fdf-5d99e465c0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+\\frac{1}{2x+3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317714} {:answer/id #uuid "d71b72d9-84d3-5862-8d6b-1d324da25f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|-\\frac{1}{2x+3}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} {:answer/id #uuid "76b2fec1-8cf1-5057-bce1-1f0399ad1d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+\\frac{3}{2x+3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317716} {:answer/id #uuid "d0822243-4d73-54a6-9195-4a9724fe3e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|-\\frac{3}{2x+3}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d71b72d9-84d3-5862-8d6b-1d324da25f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|-\\frac{1}{2x+3}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccfc7130-a98e-57de-9f14-3ddc1dffd4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a314e13d-b287-542f-9d01-bb5a501b5ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11373" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(2x+3)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+3)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)$ and get\n\n$$\n14{x}^{2}+47x+42=A(2x+3)(x+3)+B(x+3)+C(2x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+47(-\\frac{3}{2})+42 & =A(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)(-\\frac{3}{2}+3) \\\\  & +B(-\\frac{3}{2}+3)+C{(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{63}{2}-\\frac{141}{2}+42 & =B(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{63-141+84}{2} & =B(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ B & =-\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14(-3)^{2}+47(-3)+42 & =A(2(-3)+3)(-3+3)+B(-3+3)+C(2(-3)+3)^{2} \\\\ 126-141+42 & =C{(-3)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{27}{9} \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14({0}^{2})+47(0)+42 & =A(2(0)+3)(0+3)+B(0+3)+C(2(0)+3)^{2} \\\\ 42 & =9A+3B+9C \\\\ 42 & =9A+3(2)+9(3) \\\\ 42 & =9A+33 \\\\ 9A & =42-33 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{9} \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(2x+3)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(2x+3)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|-\\frac{1}{2x+3}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87928e3-ae73-5916-9b48-de246231c1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317720} {:answer/id #uuid "063b8da2-643e-508b-beae-2acb0dd19215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317721} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ed51fe-785c-56dd-a205-9b6ed0e3f36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{4}{x-2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317722} {:answer/id #uuid "28251d54-0ca6-5844-a528-f9e2a7d1d410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{4}{x-2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317723} {:answer/id #uuid "bd57a677-3b5e-57f3-b4a5-16de5b56b755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd57a677-3b5e-57f3-b4a5-16de5b56b755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6105b939-e78e-5141-8c85-e6dc7821ec4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "121bc432-4485-5cd3-bccf-f598e063c559" :question/math-academy-id "q-115323" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4x-10=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)-10 & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)-10 & =A(0-2)+B \\\\ -10 & =-2A-2 \\\\ -2A & =-8 \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{(x-2)}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{(x-2)}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-2(-\\frac{1}{x-2})+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f85b8fb5-6432-588a-8182-63109a019a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{3}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317727} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a3c53d-a365-5c65-98de-1cbbb15ad00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{1}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317728} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa78d58-4669-520a-8109-bf6693cb281f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{1}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317729} {:answer/id #uuid "0571ef11-9ad6-582a-9561-ab8ab4deab95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317730} {:answer/id #uuid "c242b7a4-a488-5cb7-83ac-33ed15177ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{4}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0571ef11-9ad6-582a-9561-ab8ab4deab95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317730} :answer-field/id #uuid "8043fe4d-6bef-52e3-b006-8b072412feef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ae7adfa-d253-53d5-ac7b-8d1dff4b65e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115537" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{x(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{x(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-2}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-12x+4=A(x-2)^{2}+Bx(x-2)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)^{2}-12(2)+4 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2)(2-2)+C(2) \\\\ -4 & =2C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}-12(0)+4 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0)(0-2)+C(0) \\\\ 4 & =4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{2}-12(1)+4 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1)(1-2)+C(1) \\\\ -4 & =A-B+C \\\\ -4 & =1-B+(-2) \\\\ -3 & =-B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d77d3b-592d-5d59-8d9b-4df33ddb1c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{3}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317734} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fabf61-f9ea-5b30-88ae-98fcf0c2834d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317735} {:answer/id #uuid "68da6f7b-a9a0-5a37-a1a4-02525bce58ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317736} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e43ff4-a13f-5326-8d40-17930e59f4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317737} {:answer/id #uuid "f3173e69-3a59-5b06-95bd-75454c7963c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68da6f7b-a9a0-5a37-a1a4-02525bce58ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317736} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78aa3c7-4eef-5807-aeb3-ceb95651772c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a7ac9ce-1b80-59f7-acbb-5c7bdeff9908" :question/math-academy-id "q-115623" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}=A(2x+1)^{2}+B(2x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =A{(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)}^{2}+B(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2} & =A(2(0)+1)^{2}+B(2(0)+1)+C \\\\ 0 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)^{2} & =A(2(-1)+1)^{2}+B(2(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=3$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c254d3a6-b98d-537f-81a7-bdb3e3f71dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|-\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317741} {:answer/id #uuid "95b294d4-9c65-5ab5-abba-fa64221d7406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|-\\frac{1}{x-6}+\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317742} {:answer/id #uuid "b47d9eb7-7154-52d6-84b7-5d7579de0a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{2}{x-6}+\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317743} {:answer/id #uuid "543a1e7f-f6f2-5988-8f27-3dba6c473604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317744} {:answer/id #uuid "0f85775a-5507-5504-ad14-6cfb9faa4a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{2}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543a1e7f-f6f2-5988-8f27-3dba6c473604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317744} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ad9f03e-f0e3-5b26-a221-402db58f9c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "563e16e6-d669-5850-92a8-f4c65daa5805" :question/math-academy-id "q-115655" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-6}+\\frac{B}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-6)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-6)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+9x-16=A(x-6)^{2}+B(x-6)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(6)^{2}+9(6)-16 & =A(6-6)^{2}+B(6-6)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=5$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(5)^{2}+9(5)-16 & =A(5-6)^{2}+B(5-6)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+2 \\\\ A-B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=7$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(7)^{2}+9(7)-16 & =A(7-6)^{2}+B(7-6)+C \\\\ -2 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=2$ and $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =2-4 \\\\ A & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+B & =-4 \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-5)^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75427d30-f5e4-5518-a8ed-00d59cf98817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317748} {:answer/id #uuid "c7839d84-98d6-549e-9933-3224deea8ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{4}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317749} {:answer/id #uuid "f997cad2-6080-5b0e-9ec6-468371ff8f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317750} {:answer/id #uuid "c65de0c3-a41e-5e27-b84f-d386da59806a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{1}{3x+1}+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317751} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2ec2b5-1dff-544b-a42f-48a79c5574b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3x+1}-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f997cad2-6080-5b0e-9ec6-468371ff8f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317750} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7bcf44-2203-5e38-8439-4840b1e2976b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03c224d9-3048-59ae-958c-8f685711017c" :question/math-academy-id "q-115656" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{3x+1}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+1=A(3x+1)^{2}+B(3x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+1 & =A{(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)}^{2}+B(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)^{2}+1 & =A(3(0)+1)^{2}+B(3(0)+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(-1)^{2}+1 & =A(3(-1)+1)^{2}+B(3(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A-2B+2 \\\\ 4A-2B & =8 \\\\ 2A-B & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $2A-B=4$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =4-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d468051c-3d51-5339-8c2c-fed4d34612a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x+8}+\\frac{2}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317755} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7a4346-af38-5afd-9c69-a66ca7659c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x+8}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317756} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfe4d16-5d30-5652-a593-5b91c33f1efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|-\\frac{2}{x+8}+\\frac{3}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317757} {:answer/id #uuid "982a5ad8-b8e5-5ab3-897c-b514b429cba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317758} {:answer/id #uuid "443e5940-84af-5c38-9022-ca957a69305e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "443e5940-84af-5c38-9022-ca957a69305e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317759} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c34d3c2-565f-5501-a65b-818bacdc77b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64afdcef-8011-5961-a509-37f146762065" :question/math-academy-id "q-115662" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+8}+\\frac{B}{(x+8)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+8)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+8)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-2=A(x+8)^{2}+B(x+8)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-8,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)-2 & =A(-8+8)^{2}+B(-8+8)+C \\\\ -10 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=-7$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)-2 & =A(-7+8)^{2}+B(-7+8)+C \\\\ -9 & =A+B-10 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-9$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)-2 & =A(-9+8)^{2}+B(-9+8)+C \\\\ -11 & =A-B-10 \\\\ A-B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-1$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-1+1 \\\\ 2A & =0 \\\\ A & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+B & =1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-10\\int \\frac{1}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0d930c3-673d-53db-9ab2-8865c64a1066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\ln(2)-\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317762} {:answer/id #uuid "64d86e5b-7946-5f65-a9c7-4262a6ecdbf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d930c3-673d-53db-9ab2-8865c64a1066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\ln(2)-\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317762} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d45098-6cdb-536b-9dcd-f3aa38454270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd5af66b-b2ec-535a-9378-0690b5352eba" :question/math-academy-id "q-346710" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+2=A(x-4)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2 & =A(4-4)+B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0-4)+6 \\\\ 2 & =-4A+6 \\\\ 4A & =4 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{6}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4|)-(\\frac{6}{2-4}-\\frac{6}{1-4}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-3-(-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3197950d-1c10-5e62-b731-ec6f02a394ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(3)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3197950d-1c10-5e62-b731-ec6f02a394ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(3)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c2579b-cee5-5efa-9281-33c5279a3c71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f96ef11-e2cc-5a01-bc43-3b896b801eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-346752" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-4=A(x-1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4 & =A(1-1)+B \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4 & =A(0-1)+B \\\\ -4 & =-A-3 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|\\,{|}_{2}^{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)+3(\\frac{1}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{1})+3(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb88a5b0-67c9-5ca1-b212-be1346dc1e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-\\ln(5)+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb88a5b0-67c9-5ca1-b212-be1346dc1e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-\\ln(5)+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317769} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c1f80e9-ddac-59be-9b61-a84416ac794c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c220092-846f-5eac-8c3f-776c702f7e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-348350" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-5}+\\frac{B}{(x-5)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-5)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+3=A(x-5)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+3 & =A(5-5)+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+3 & =A(0-5)+8 \\\\ 3 & =-5A+8 \\\\ 5A & =5 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-5}+\\frac{8}{(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x-5}+\\frac{8}{(x-5)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{8}{x-5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-5|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0-5|)-(\\frac{8}{1-5}-\\frac{8}{0-5}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)-(-2-(-\\frac{8}{5})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})+\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e852ee88-acdf-5dcd-b07e-b239ab122820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(6)-\\ln(5)-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e852ee88-acdf-5dcd-b07e-b239ab122820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(6)-\\ln(5)-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317772} :answer-field/id #uuid "0552638d-bdad-58ba-be81-ace0a565fd45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcd59b71-fa7a-559a-acad-1450e2b093f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-348426" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+5}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+5)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+5)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+1 & =A(-5+5)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+5)-4 \\\\ 1 & =5A-4 \\\\ 5A & =5 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+5}-\\frac{4}{(x+5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x+5}-\\frac{4}{(x+5)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{4}{x+5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+5|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+5|)+(\\frac{4}{1+5}-\\frac{4}{0+5}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)+(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})-\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a23ce9b4-4fa6-53f0-a3c5-7ee5114d937f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(3)+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317775} {:answer/id #uuid "94d2adad-5115-5fb1-abac-1a26125f244e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a23ce9b4-4fa6-53f0-a3c5-7ee5114d937f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(3)+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317775} :answer-field/id #uuid "a84a7c1a-381f-5ecf-8c7a-54d3630dcb7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "442912a0-d2e7-5b68-8200-12d7316f04a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-348473" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n8-x=A(x-4)^{2}+Bx(x-4)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-0 & =A(0-4)^{2}+B(0)(0-4)+C(0) \\\\ 8 & =16A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-4 & =A(4-4)^{2}+B(4)(4-4)+C(4) \\\\ 4 & =4C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-1 & =A(1-4)^{2}+B(1)(1-4)+C(1) \\\\ 7 & =9A-3B+C \\\\ 7 & =9(\\frac{1}{2})-3B+1 \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2(x-4)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2(x-4)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3c36196-c673-5b59-93f2-909cd5d56d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3c36196-c673-5b59-93f2-909cd5d56d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317779} :answer-field/id #uuid "dededca6-bf82-57a0-b7cf-d7a877f561e5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a562073f-476e-5423-a33d-a22e612a68b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-348521" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2-x}{{x}^{2}-12x+36}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{{x}^{2}-12x+36}=\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-6}+\\frac{B}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-6)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n2-x=A(x-6)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-6 & =A(6-6)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-0 & =A(0-6)-4 \\\\ 2 & =-6A-4 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}(-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|\\,{|}_{2}^{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+4(-1-(-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+4(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d18e8131-971f-5aa5-923b-0169ac81f6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\ln(2)-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317782} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3a673b-e8e4-5aa8-9bfd-aee89ad5dc50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d18e8131-971f-5aa5-923b-0169ac81f6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\ln(2)-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317782} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b760fa9-8473-53aa-bd5b-e6fb7a2c0288" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae9f08bc-be36-5848-9bd0-b853f4db7daf" :question/math-academy-id "q-348617" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}+8x+16}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{{x}^{2}+8x+16}=\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+4}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n3x+7=A(x+4)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-4)+7 & =A(-4+4)+B \\\\ B & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+7 & =A(0+4)-5 \\\\ 7 & =4A-5 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{3}{x+4}-\\frac{5}{(x+4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{3x+7}{(x+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-6}^{-5}(\\frac{3}{x+4}-\\frac{5}{(x+4)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|\\,{|}_{-6}^{-5}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,{|}_{-6}^{-5} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+5(-1-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+5(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e143f29f-94b2-52da-918d-7f17de7556a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|+\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317786} {:answer/id #uuid "fed245c7-2d13-5533-b747-3df5d69d3b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{2}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317787} {:answer/id #uuid "e6dadb2c-c0ef-5696-b687-585997eedb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317788} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9dcecf-b626-5f68-a905-9571dacd9e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|+\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317789} {:answer/id #uuid "05825022-1d62-5bc3-a9a8-4bdc604a2133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{1}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6dadb2c-c0ef-5696-b687-585997eedb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{9}{t+1}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317788} :answer-field/id #uuid "8af71650-5e17-5946-89ae-00df8d17eae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7a5ae82-e9e5-5bd4-bec7-af8bae54592e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3612" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t}\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{t}+\\frac{B}{t+1}+\\frac{C}{(t+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $t(t+1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n5{t}^{2}+20t+6=A(t+1)^{2}+B(t)(t+1)+C(t).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)^{2}+20(0)+6 & =A(0+1)^{2}+B(0)(0+1)+C(0) \\\\ 6 & =A \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $t=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-1)^{2}+20(-1)+6 & =A(-1+1)^{2}+B(-1)(-1+1)+C(-1) \\\\ -9 & =-C \\\\ C & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $t=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+20(1)+6 & =A(1+1)^{2}+B(1)(1+1)+C(1) \\\\ 31 & =4A+2B+C \\\\ 31 & =4(6)+2B+(9) \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}=\\frac{6}{t}-\\frac{1}{t+1}+\\frac{9}{(t+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{t(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{(6)}{t}+\\frac{(-1)}{t+1}+\\frac{(9)}{(t+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t+9\\int \\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|-\\frac{9}{t+1}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c723e5ef-65d6-5e48-a38b-01f857c855fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{23}{6}\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317793} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6cdf46-25f3-5209-b7ef-e0a36cea9655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{23}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317794} {:answer/id #uuid "f5883cd4-3498-55c5-8a68-4af727b1a500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317795} {:answer/id #uuid "919a00ab-be13-5d98-bb76-9364a21eda90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{5}{3x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317796} {:answer/id #uuid "183d8c82-a5ec-5886-ab3f-7c95273e4857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{6}{23}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce6cdf46-25f3-5209-b7ef-e0a36cea9655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{23}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317794} :answer-field/id #uuid "38348bb2-28f3-54cf-a187-5e498cfc4669" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "145e4701-d43b-5056-a42b-a1c651706ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49785" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}=-\\frac{4}{3x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{23}{3(2x+3)},\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{(2x+3){x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We rewrite our integral and integrate term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{3x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{23}{3(2x+3)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{23}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{23}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d994a69f-f77a-577b-a7ed-c79e3e4a31d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317800} {:answer/id #uuid "a87e02af-54ae-55ef-b15b-efee1401452b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317801} {:answer/id #uuid "58126e72-b979-5d73-916c-81500fa394ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317802} {:answer/id #uuid "aaef68f1-372c-5f9f-a35d-3d21f85080f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317803} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b4d31f-9a9c-5038-ae39-809d7ff481ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d994a69f-f77a-577b-a7ed-c79e3e4a31d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317800} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe59d3fe-bee7-5f4b-b675-cb8f51b9df58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f4beeb0-a230-5a24-9f4a-0465c9611ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-49902" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4x=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2) & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ 8 & =0+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0) & =A(0-2)+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+8 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|{|}_{3}^{4}+8(-\\frac{1}{x-2}){|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8(-\\frac{1}{4-2}+\\frac{1}{3-2}) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1abd3c48-8bbc-5329-a336-e492860d1a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid - (6)/((x + 1)^{2}) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068280} {:answer/id #uuid "488bd9be-167f-5ffc-9d27-53bf9f60a7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid + \\frac{2}{x + 1} + (3)/((x + 1)^{2}) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068281} {:answer/id #uuid "48a1d677-a958-5482-bf5e-1387393c80a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid - \\frac{3}{x + 1} + (1)/((x + 1)^{2}) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068282} {:answer/id #uuid "4a457e5c-36a1-5f2c-8d53-2abe6943e5fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x + 1} + (3)/((x + 1)^{2}) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068283} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc543ea-4e3d-5dc4-916a-f548f5432d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x + 1} + (1)/((x + 1)^{2}) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cc543ea-4e3d-5dc4-916a-f548f5432d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x + 1} + (1)/((x + 1)^{2}) + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068284} :answer-field/id #uuid "0838ec9e-2f1c-511e-8808-cff1da71d6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1af827e0-397a-561e-854f-4bfe4e074477" :question/math-academy-id "q-82892" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n3x+1=A(x+1)^{2}+B(x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+1 & =A(-1+1)^{2}+B(-1+1)+C \\\\ -2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+1 & =A(0+1)^{2}+B(0+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B-2 \\\\ A+B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-2$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)+1 & =A(-2+1)^{2}+B(-2+1)+C \\\\ -5 & =A-B-2 \\\\ A-B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-3$ and $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-3+3 \\\\ 2A & =0 \\\\ A & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+B & =3 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}]}
